::: {.doc-subject}

Check if the graph is regular (all nodes have the same degree).
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L28188-L28309){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.is_regular()
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Is regular](../assets/graphs/is-regular.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Left: a six-node cycle; the badges show every node has degree 2, so is_regular() returns True. Right: the same cycle plus a chord between C and F (shown in the accent color), which raises C and F to degree 3 while the rest stay at 2, so is_regular() returns False." width="100%"}

A graph is regular if every node has the same degree. For directed graphs, this means all
nodes have the same in-degree and the same out-degree (though in-degree and out-degree don't
need to equal each other).

Degrees are computed from all edges: parallel edges each count, and in an undirected graph
a self-loop adds `2` to its node's degree (in a directed graph it adds `1` to both the
in-degree and out-degree). Isolated nodes have degree `0`, so a single isolated node makes
an otherwise regular graph irregular. Use `is_k_regular()` when you need a specific
degree rather than just a uniform one.

Regularity is a useful structural check when:

- verifying that a generated lattice, ring, or other synthetic network is as uniform as
  intended
- checking the assumptions of results that only hold for regular graphs (e.g. certain
  spectral bounds)
- confirming that degree-based centrality can't distinguish any nodes (every node gets
  the same score in a regular graph)

The whole graph is checked and the current selection is ignored.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   `True` if the graph is regular, `False` otherwise. Graphs with zero or one node are
    regular.
:::

## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}

Empty graphs and single-node graphs pass vacuously, cycles are $2$-regular, and the
complete graph $K_n$ is $(n - 1)$-regular. For directed graphs, both in-degree and
out-degree must be constant across nodes, though the constants themselves don't need
to agree, so a $k$-in-$\ell$-out schema is still regular. Regularity is the hypothesis
behind many spectral shortcuts: the adjacency eigenvalue $\lambda_1$ equals the shared
degree, and expander bounds like Cheeger's inequality assume it.

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~is_k_regular`) 

:   Whether every node has degree k.

[](`~degrees`) 

:   Get the degree of every node.

[](`~degree_distribution`) 

:   Count the nodes of each degree.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

Every node in a cycle has exactly two neighbors, so cycles are always regular. A star's hub
connects to every other node while each spoke connects only to the hub, so it isn't:

::: {#e6f25a0c .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

cycle = ec.Graph().add_cycle_graph(5)
star = ec.Graph().add_star_graph(4)

cycle.is_regular(), star.is_regular()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
(True, False)
```
:::
:::


In a directed cycle every node has one incoming and one outgoing edge, so it is regular. A
directed path is not: its first node has no incoming edge and its last node no outgoing
edge:

::: {#67498fa3 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
directed_cycle = ec.Graph(directed=True).add_cycle_graph(n=4)
directed_path = ec.Graph(directed=True).add_path_graph(n=4)

directed_cycle.is_regular(), directed_path.is_regular()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
(True, False)
```
:::
:::


### Rings of atoms

In a molecule's graph, with the hydrogens left out, each carbon in benzene's ring has two
neighbors, so the graph is regular. Caffeine's atoms have one, two or three:

::: {#4ed0c63f .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
molecules = ec.molecules()
benzene, caffeine = molecules["benzene"], molecules["caffeine"]


def degrees(graph):
    return sorted(set(graph.degrees(return_dict=True).values()))


(
    (benzene.is_regular(), degrees(benzene)),
    (caffeine.is_regular(), degrees(caffeine)),
)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}
```
((True, [2]), (False, [1, 2, 3]))
```
:::
:::


